/** chokhmat-shlomo seifim 1-29 for siman 017 */
export const T = {
  "1#_": `Seif 1 — A married woman is among the forbidden relations, etc. See Beit Shmuel s.k. 3 what he wrote in the name of the Rashba responsum 1213 — that if she said first not in her husband's presence she can afterward say in her husband's presence and be believed; see what I wondered on his proof from chapter 2 of Ketubot in my composition Turei Zahav on Yoreh De'ah edition 6 laws of treifot siman 36 and in my composition siman 55 in responsum 130 incidentally — see there and examine.`,
  "2#_": `There in seif 1. See Beit Shmuel brought in the name of Maharival that Elijah's wife or R' Yehudah bar Liva'i is permitted, for she is not called a married woman but an angel's wife; and in my humble opinion this does not appear so — for if so there is difficulty on the plain sugya in chapter 4 of Eruvin that there are techumim above ten handbreadths from the baraita "I am a nazir on the day Ben David comes," etc.; and afterward it challenges there on one Shabbat to permit, and what proof from Elijah — behold among the dead he is free and is exempt from all mitzvot; also what did Rabbah challenge in chapter HaMekabel in Bava Metzia — "my master is not a kohen," behold he is an angel and is not obligated in fulfillment of Torah — perforce since he did not die he is judged as a person as if alive and is considered a person and not an angel, and automatically his wife is forbidden; and this is clear proof in my humble opinion. And see what I wrote in the sugya of Eruvin there in my composition on Orach Chaim laws of Eruvin part 95 — see there and examine.`,
  "3#_": `Seif 2 — The woman who said to her husband in his presence "you divorced me," etc. I was asked: why when she says "you divorced me" is she believed based on the presumption that a woman does not act brazenly, etc. — behold there are against it two presumptions: presumption of Torah prohibition and presumption of money for those who hold she collects her ketubah thereby; if so it is one against two. And even though the presumption that a woman does not act brazenly is a presumption that comes from reasoning and is preferable to other presumptions — nevertheless there is no reasoning to say it is more preferable than two presumptions. The questioner asked and I answered him: the difficulty here is only for one opinion; for regarding death she certainly collects her ketubah, and Tosafot challenged regarding one who had relations with her on Yom Kippur — behold he stands for a sin-offering, for two against two is rabbinic safek and from Torah we establish on presumption; and they answered: she examines and marries, etc. Difficulty: behold here too are two presumptions — also presumption of money — and still he stands for a sin-offering; strained to say she examines for two presumptions. Further difficulty: regarding falling in endless waters all wrote she leaves Torah prohibition for majority indicate death, and there is an opinion she is completely permitted — why does she take her ketubah thereby, and we do not follow money after majority? But in truth there is no beginning to this difficulty — for prohibitions and monetary matters are separate matters and do not combine; similar to what we hold in Choshen Mishpat siman 87 — where the essence of the claim is because of prohibition, even though there is practical difference for money, nevertheless he is exempt from oath; behold we follow the essence of the claim and do not regard what follows automatically. So too here one may say: behold when claiming ketubah she is not believed; only when not claiming she is believed to say "you divorced me," and the essence of the claim is because of prohibition — one should not judge what will follow afterward regarding money (the Maharach said a word of truth in his mouth, and so wrote the author of Pela'ah in his novellae end of chapter HaIshah ShenitArmelah and the Rosh). And see Yevamot 93b — R' Shimon asked regarding one witness with a yevamah; and in Tosafot there s.v. one witness with a yevamah until Rava said one witness is believed with a yevamah from a kal vechomer; and Rashi explained there and Tosafot wondered on him; and in my humble opinion one may say the Gemara certainly knew the reason one witness is believed is because of dayika uminsaba — only it needed to ask whether prohibitions and monetary matters are one matter and combine, or are separate matters and do not combine; therefore if here it is one matter — behold there is presumption of Torah prohibition also presumption of money — therefore one witness alone without dayika cannot reject two presumptions; perforce the reason is because of almoni and one presumption is rejected by avdei legluyei and one by dayika uminsaba; automatically with a yevamah where there is no presumption of money — obligation of ketubah applies either way when her husband died — therefore the reason of almoni alone suffices; or perhaps prohibition and money do not combine and there is only one presumption alone and dayika suffices; but almoni does not help to reject the presumption — therefore with a yevamah where dayika is inapplicable she is forbidden; and thus Tosafot's difficulty s.v. one witness with a yevamah is resolved; therefore Rava holds simply that prohibitions and money combine and there are two presumptions; and although we also need dayika — nevertheless we need almoni and it is proven the reason of almoni helps to overturn one presumption; therefore with a yevamah one reason of almoni alone suffices; therefore he says kal vechomer for prohibition of karet he permitted; and Rashi is settled — examine and understand, for I shortened. And behold what I wrote that with a yevamah there is no presumption of money — this is even though if she performs yibum she has no ketubah immediately; nevertheless as long as she did not perform yibum the yavam did not acquire his dead brother's property — if so he and the yavam are equal in them and he has no greater presumption than she to establish money on her presumption; on the contrary she has a clear debt and he has only safek if he wishes to perform yibum — therefore the property is not in his presumption more than hers. Examine and examine. And behold what I wrote above that prohibition and money do not combine — do not reject this from what we say at the end of Yevamot that R' Akiva holds witnesses for women do not require examination because there is ketubah to collect — it is similar to what the questioner above wanted to reject; but in truth it is not similar — for there from Torah even for money we require examination, only the rabbis enacted we do not require examination in such a case; therefore if it can be said ab initio the rabbis did not divide in their enactment — it depends on this: if there is also practical difference for money — since there is practical difference also for money it is in the category of such a case and one can say the rabbis did not divide in their enactment; but if there is no practical difference for money at all — it is inapplicable to say they did not divide, for it is not in the category of the enactment. But regarding Torah certainly prohibition and money do not combine — so is correct and plain; examine.`,
  "4#_": `Seif 3 — A woman whose husband went to a distant land and they testified he died, etc. Behold in Yevamot (93b) it is asked regarding testimony of death whether it is because of almoni or because of dayika; and I wondered: why is it not resolved from there — behold the view of Rav Acha in chapter 1 of Sanhedrin that from Torah even one is fit to judge; how is one witness believed to extract money? I already challenged thus in my composition Turei Zahav on Yoreh De'ah 100 laws of bekhor in responsum 130 and wrote the reason is because of almoni; if so it is proven that for money one witness is believed by almoni; behold a matter of forbidden relations is derived matter from matter from money; if so here too he is believed by almoni. This is a question; one may say the Gemara's questions refer to that opinion in Sanhedrin that from Torah requires three; with this it is a question. Rambam ruled that the question was resolved; and it is true per what I wrote in several responsa already that not every matter of almoni is equal — there is almoni that helps from Torah as in ashtamud'inanhu, and there is not almoni immediately such as woman's testimony — here it does not help from Torah; yet it is not settled that in chapter 4 of Yevamot R' Eliezer and Ravina dispute regarding ashtamud'inanhu; and if two are required why does it not say it is proven R' Eliezer holds one suffices, for he holds one judges from Torah by almoni; and it is proven in any case where almoni immediately helps with one — how can one think here two are required; strained to say rabbinically two are required, for regarding woman's testimony the rabbis were lenient and from Torah we say they were more stringent here; behold how many leniencies they were lenient regarding woman's testimony. Further difficulty for Rivash who holds one does not help in ashtamud'inanhu except if they testify before the act and not at the time of the act — and from Torah one suffices; strained to say Rivash follows the opinion that requires three from Torah — uncertain in all this. And behold Tosafot there s.v. one witness with a yevamah — even according to the opinion that because of almoni one also needs the reason of dayika; only it is asked whether a small dayika suffices or not — see there; and apparently it lacks reason: if because of almoni he is believed from Torah — why need the reason of dayika? But the intent is per what we learn in Bekhorot (36b) there — since he has a claimant he trembles; it is proven explicitly there that one must distinguish regarding almoni between there is a claimant or not; therefore here too if dayika is applicable almoni helps, but if dayika is inapplicable almoni does not help. And behold one may say Rambam's words that he wrote the reason one witness is believed is because of almoni — all wondered on him, for in the Gemara it is an unresolved question; per the above it appears Rambam derived this from the sugya of Bekhorot where there are two versions for one version almoni helps even without a claimant, and for one version almoni does not help except where there is a claimant; and so too should be the Gemara's question in Yevamot whether the reason one witness is believed is because of almoni even without dayika like one version in Bekhorot that even without a claimant almoni helps, or only because of dayika — because there is a claimant almoni helps but without a claimant almoni does not help; and the Gemara's question is whether all like the first version in Bekhorot or the second; and if so since the Gemara concludes in Bekhorot the halakhah is like the first version that almoni helps without a claimant — we hold also one witness for a woman that almoni helps even without dayika; and from this Rambam derived thus. And in truth in Tosafot Bekhorot there they sensed from woman's testimony that almoni does not help except because of dayika; and in my humble opinion from this Rambam derived his ruling — we hold like avdei legluyei — examine well. And behold on what is asked in the Gemara there, etc., and we rule stringently — see in my edition 9 in my responsum to the country of Russia in my composition siman 219 where I wrote something new to distinguish, with God's help, where the witness is from a nearby place where it appears to court he would be shamed if the opposite were revealed — then from Torah he would be believed because of almoni, only rabbinically he is not believed because we decree lest it be a distant place; therefore because of dayika they established it on Torah and he is believed even rabbinically; but if the witness is in a distant place where it appears to court he would not be shamed if his falsehood were revealed — then from Torah he is not believed at all, only rabbinically he is believed because of dayika; and there is great practical difference between Torah prohibition and rabbinic — as I wrote there with God's help; see and examine. Behold with one witness for a husband's death there are opinions whether it is from Torah or rabbinically; and I saw in Sefer Neta Sha'ashuim part Even HaEzer siman 62 that he challenged those who hold it is from Torah — and witness from witness is certainly rabbinic; if so why do we say in Shevuot that all agree regarding testimony of death he is exempt — for he spoke to her and not to court; behold in gambling with dice since from Torah he is fit he is liable for an oath; if so here too from Torah her statement is ineffective — to obligate an oath see there; and in my humble opinion leave it in uncertainty; and in truth after examination there is no beginning to his difficulty — for there in gambling with dice since now they do not collect this debt since there are no witnesses — ultimately the court loses through him by his not testifying; therefore although rabbinically disqualified, since from Torah he is fit he is liable for an oath; but with a woman — since in any case rabbinically she is believed to say — she can go to court to testify and collect her ketubah; therefore she will not ultimately lose money through him; therefore even from Torah there is no oath liability here — it is as if it is known the borrower will pay him without witnesses — the oath is only denial of the matter in general; so too here — examine well. And what I wrote above in seif 2 "but where there is one to support her," etc. — see Beit Shmuel who brought a dispute on this; and see in my composition on Choshen Mishpat edition 3 in responsum 130 to Kolinits in Choshen Mishpat siman 87 what I wrote as proof for those who hold even with one witness she does not act brazenly — see there where I extended on this and proved from the Gemara and Tosafot with God's help; examine.`,
  "5#_": `Seif 3 — A woman, etc., and they testified he died, etc. Behold I saw in Sefer Neta Sha'ashuim (siman 62) that he wants to prove testimony of death is rabbinic — for if from Torah, behold witness from witness is certainly rabbinic; if so how does the Gemara say in chapter 4 of Shevuot that all agree regarding testimony of death he is exempt when he spoke to her and not to court; behold the Gemara says gambling with dice is liable for an oath from the power of from Torah they see fit; if so here too from Torah his testimony is needed — he left it in uncertainty. And behold after examination he erred and compared two separate matters — oath of testimony applies only if his testimony is needed; but if his testimony is not needed, oath of testimony is inapplicable. Therefore in gambling with dice his testimony is needed, for without his testimony in truth the claimant is not paid; therefore since his testimony is needed, only we say even if he testifies it will not help here — we follow Torah law and from Torah he is fit for testimony and oath of testimony applies since his testimony is needed. But where now his testimony is not needed — for ultimately now the rabbis established her and if so without his testimony she may marry; and since in truth his testimony is not needed here — certainly we follow rabbinic law lest from another side he does not need testimony since the other pays automatically or as if other witnesses came for this or as if she does not seek witnesses at all; and wherever his testimony is not needed we follow rabbinic law. But where his testimony is needed only there is no benefit to his testimony here — we follow Torah law — examine well. See in my responsum on laws of yibum siman 169 what I proved they did not lighten for a woman because of takkanat ha'ishut except where the witness testifies on actual death and not on another matter; but for the law — uncertain; for in my responsum already to Zlatopol I proved the opposite and so is the implication of the poskim — uncertain in this now; perhaps God will merit me in time to speak at length on this; examine.`,
  "6#_": `In that seif — And likewise, etc., apostate, etc., if he speaks offhandedly he is believed. See whether this is so also for an apostate to provoke or not; see in my edition 8 in my responsum 130 to Dubna, acquired place, it is laws of Gittin siman 120, and in my composition siman 69 — see there what I wrote with God's help; examine.`,
  "7#_": `Seif 4 — All are believed to testify for her, etc. Behold in my composition edition 4 siman 21 I brought a safek in the name of another sage — perhaps Chazal did not believe one witness for a husband's death except if he testifies on actual death and not on other details of the matter; and I proved there there is no distinction in the matter; however it appears this is specifically if there is no testimony of witnesses or women and disqualified persons against the presumption of Torah prohibition; or one may say on the contrary it is preferable if they do not testify on actual presumption of Torah prohibition; and in any case it is not worse; therefore since a woman dayika uminsaba examines for presumption of Torah prohibition — Chazal believed them. But if they testify against the actual presumption — such as one came before us and established his name Yosef son of Shimon and married a woman, and afterward wandered and left his wife an agunah, and his wife heard from guests and youths that he changed his name and his name was Reuven Ba'al Milchamah [as in the incident that came before me responsum to Premishla] — and afterward came one witness that Reuven Ba'al Milchamah died — in this certainly she is forbidden, since there is no clear testimony before us that he changed his name, only she says so and she too did not hear from witnesses in court; if so since he was established before us by another name — this testimony that he changed his name is against the presumption; therefore it is like two presumptions of Torah prohibition and presumption that his name was established as Yosef son of Shimon; if so for one presumption the reasoning of dayika has power to harm the presumption; but for two presumptions one presumption that a woman dayika does not have power to diminish both; therefore we say the presumption of dayika is removed for presumption of married woman — still remains presumption that his name was established thus and so; and they are believed only with fit witnesses. Do not add to this; and particularly since this is the beginning of testimony — behold we say in Bekhorot that at the beginning of testimony Chazal were more stringent regarding married woman than generally — all the more we do not lighten here more and require clear testimony on this — so is correct in my humble opinion and there is no place to permit the woman in this; examine.`,
  "8#_": `There in the same seif — Except for five women. See in my edition 3 in my responsum 130 to Bilyakamin what I wrote on these matters; and in explanation of the language of Yerushalmi in the mishnah "my husband died and afterward my son died" — many matters belonging to these laws; and in my composition in Mahadura Kama I also extended on the law of mother-in-law who comes afterward — examine. And behold if the daughter of her co-wife who has a husband from another man, or daughter of her husband's brother and the like — is she permitted to testify for her; or daughter of her mother-in-law who comes afterward — see in my edition 3 in my responsum 130 to Zinkowitz in this section seif 24 what I wrote that all are believed — see there in his reason; and see in Gevurat Pinchas who was uncertain in this; and in my humble opinion it is clear all are believed — see there what I extended with God's help; examine. And behold the later authorities brought the opinion of Shulchan Gavoah that males similar to these are believed; and in my opinion uncertain from the mishnah in Gittin 23 that taught there "even the women who are not believed to testify can bring her get — her mother-in-law," etc.; difficulty: why specify those; granted in Yevamot it teaches which women are not believed; but here that says "even the women who are not believed to testify on her husband's death can bring her get" — automatically we know what they are from the mishnah in Yevamot; therefore it would appear the opposite — one may say males all the more so they are not believed, since the reason one witness for death is believed is because of dayika; if so one may say with males testifying the woman should not fear damage and they will not be suspected in her eyes to lie; therefore do not infer all this; but with women who hate her — since women's minds are light and she herself will fear they intend her damage — therefore infer properly and she will be believed because of dayika uminsaba; therefore it teaches "even the women are not believed" and all the more the males. Per this one may say that although Chazal said the women who are not believed to say her husband died are believed to bring her get — one may say specifically females; and according to what Tosafot wrote in Ketubot 26b that also in divorce dayika applies lest witnesses be arranged — one may say here too writing alone does not help except combined with dayika that she fears lest they testify to damage her; therefore dayika and combined with the writing proves we rely on dayika uminsaba; but with males who are not suspected in her eyes to lie out of hatred — no dayika; therefore even for a get they are disqualified; therefore the tanna of the Gemara specifies women to exclude males who are not believed. However in my opinion it is correct — I have no power to innovate what is not mentioned in any posek; and particularly one must bring proof to the contrary for the opinion of Shulchan Gavoah from what is asked in the Gemara regarding mother-in-law who comes afterward; and the Gemara resolves from what the tanna taught five women — behold there are six; and the Gemara rejects — still the count, why need the count; and since it counts and continues — why need the count; and similarly challenges in the first chapter of Yevamot and several places; perforce it will be proven it excludes males; therefore the tanna taught the count; and it is proven males are believed; if so uncertain on the mishnah in Gittin why it specified the names of the women — uncertain now; examine. And see further in my composition Mahadura Zayin siman 181 in my responsum where I wrote proof to the contrary also from the words of Shulchan Gavoah; and I also raised that males too are not believed, and also in ashtamud'inanhu the five women are not believed nor males similar to them — see there with God's help; examine.`,
  "9#_": `Seif 5 — We already said that the witness who said, etc., behold he is fit, etc. Behold in law if one witness is believed from Torah for the reason therefore they believed him because of stringency, etc. — Tosafot's view is not from Torah but rabbinically; see in Tosafot 88; but see what I wrote in my composition on Orach Chaim part the inscribed one and ninety page 130 in responsum 130 Bitcash and laws of etrog where I raised one may say through the reasoning of dayika it becomes of Torah and he is believed from Torah and because of presumption a person does not sin and not for his own benefit — see there in his reason, for it is correct with God's help; examine.`,
  "10#_": `Seif 5 — And they should not eulogize him until they know he died, etc. See Beit Shmuel what he wrote that if we saw him expiring three days he certainly died; and see in the later authorities who sensed this and dispute him; and see in my composition on Yoreh De'ah edition 7 siman 205 in laws of mourning siman 339 what I wrote to uphold this law with God's help — see there and examine.`,
  "11#_": `Seif 7 — One came and said I killed him, etc. See what I wrote somewhat in the sugya — source of this law in Yevamot 22; see what I wrote in responsum on Choshen Mishpat edition 4 siman 121 in laws of testimony siman 34 and in responsum 130 in Be'er in my edition 7 — see there and examine.`,
  "12#_": `Seif 10 — They heard a voice, etc. Behold what the Shulchan Aruch stated here contradicts the law of Gittin siman 141 regarding one who was cast in a pit, etc.; see what I wrote in my edition 8 siman 69 in responsum 130 Dubna acquired place in laws of Gittin siman 120 — see there with God's help; examine.`,
  "13#_": `Seif 14 in the gloss — And likewise if he said so-and-so died speaking offhandedly, etc., is called properly mesalfei lefi tumo, etc. Behold although there is no proof for the matter, there is remembrance of the matter — for mesalfei lefi tumo applies even without connecting words in Bekhorot (35a) in what is stated not to bring mesalfei lefi tumo; and Rashi explained they did not ask the butcher what its nature is but they themselves speak offhandedly and say "see, do not slaughter this ever except through a blemish" and they did not intend thereby to inflict a blemish on it — from this it is proven even without connecting words whatever is not through a question is mesalfei lefi tumo; and this is clear proof in my humble opinion; examine. And see in my composition Turei Zahav on Yoreh De'ah edition 6 siman 202 in my responsum laws of bekhor where I wrote proof to the contrary that mesalfei lefi tumo without connecting words does not help from the Gemara Bava Kamma 113b — see there with God's help; examine.`,
  "14#_": `Seif 15 — A gentile who spoke offhandedly, etc. See in Beit Shmuel s.k. 41 who brought opinions whether it requires in that same setting or not; and I in my edition 8 in my responsum on laws of Gittin in my composition siman 69 wrote it appears to me it requires specifically in that same setting. And behold now it appears to me proof for this from the Gemara Bava Kamma (114b) where Rav Ashi says mesalfei lefi tumo is fit only for woman's testimony alone; Ravina said to R' Elazar — but behold a swarm of bees, etc.; and afterward challenges there further from an incident with a certain man, etc.; and why does it challenge — let us say R' Elazar's intent that for woman's testimony it is fit means even if once he was mesalfei lefi tumo then he is fit even if after days he tells through a question; but for other prohibitions it does not help if he is not mesalfei lefi tumo in that same setting; but with a swarm of bees and with this one who left the school — since it is in that same setting he is fit even for Torah law; perforce if not in that same setting it is not reasonable to say he would be called mesalfei lefi tumo and be fit for woman's testimony; and this is clear proof in my humble opinion with God's help; examine well.`,
  "15#_": `In that seif in the gloss — And they told him about the Jew whether he is alive or dead, etc. See Beit Shmuel s.k. 58 who wrote similarly if the Jew said Reuven died it is as if he said Reuven from my city died since he said "the Jew"; see in my composition on Orach Chaim part the inscribed 102 page 28b belonging to Orach Chaim siman 256 where I proved from the Gemara Gittin 14 in the question of R' Shimon the Nasi or in the name of the Nasi — this is a question in the Gemara and remains in uncertainty and safek lechumra — see there what I wrote with God's help; examine.`,
  "16#_": `Seif 16 in the gloss — And some dispute this. Behold students of tractate Gittin 28 — on what it challenges there "what died and what was killed" — let us say died literally and killed literally as with gentile counter-missionaries; why do they not permit his wife; behold it is mesalfei lefi tumo; and what does it challenge — behold there is no connecting words there; afterward I saw in the commentators who brought this proof from there — Rivash himself; but after examination in my opinion it can be rejected. One may say the essence of the reason connecting words are required is to prove he is not lying and his intention is not to testify; if we knew he speaks truth it would help even if not mesalfei lefi tumo; and the essence of the concern is lest he lies; and if he says connecting words it is proven he is not lying; if so likewise if there is other proof he is not lying it is like connecting words. If so when saying "killed in courts" — why need to say this, for it is an uncommon expression; behold according to this opinion the Gemara did not know the reason avdei le'achzukei shakranei; if so why need to say an uncommon expression more than a common expression to say simply "died"; from this it is proven he speaks truth and it is like connecting words; perforce the Ran does not require connecting words except when saying simply "died" — there is concern for lying; but when saying "died" or "killed in courts" he does not require connecting words, for it is proven he is not lying and it is like a miggo — if he were lying he would say simply "died." And there is proof for this from the language of the baraita itself that taught he said "died" or "killed" — what difference to me "died" or "killed"; why did it teach "killed" — perforce as amplification for what is stated that even though he says "killed" which is uncommon, nevertheless at the beginning he is believed and at the end he is not believed. Granted in Yevamot where it taught there "died," "I killed him," "we killed him" — there it teaches even though he makes himself wicked and the dispute of R' Yehudah and the rabbis there; but here why need "killed" — perforce as amplification; if so one may say for this reason itself he does not require connecting words, since it is proven he is not lying he does not require connecting words. And similarly wrote Rama regarding disqualified witnesses if it is proven without deceit — and through rumor their testimony helps; so too one may say here. And with this one can resolve what Tosafot challenged there — why did it not challenge from the end that they do not permit his wife; and they answered one may say here mesalfei lefi tumo does not help — but it lacks reason; but in my opinion one may say what the Gemara states "rather what was killed — going out to be killed" — the intent is not that he says so explicitly, but the intent is thus: since he says simply "died" or "killed" and since the one who says so is among counter-missionaries — there is concern perhaps his intent is going out to be killed; therefore with an Israelite either way she is permitted; but with gentiles there is concern since he is among counter-missionaries perhaps he saw him going out to kill him — therefore he says in a manner of doubt "was killed"; if so it is a question: since in truth connecting words are required, only since he says "killed" in an uncommon expression it is proven he is not lying — if he were lying he would say simply "died" in a common expression; if so it works if he says already "died" or "killed"; but if his intent is to say he was going out to kill — again there is no proof from what he says "killed"; one may say he lies and fears perhaps afterward it will be revealed he lied; therefore he does not say simply "died" but says going out to be killed; if so he would not be established as a liar, for he can excuse himself that afterward they released him and found merit for him; if so if he excuses that his intent was he was going out to be killed — properly he mentions preferably "killed" in this which is more common among counter-missionaries than "died" to throw him to the furnace as Rashi explained; if so there is no proof from his language that he did not say simply "died" since his intent was not to be caught as a liar; and again since there is no proof from his words — again mesalfei lefi tumo does not help since there are no connecting words; and there would be proof from this on the contrary that connecting words are generally required — examine well. And further one can reject Rivash's proofs simply — one may say with two mesalfei lefi tumo connecting words are not required, for each one is like legs to the matter for the second; as I wrote in responsum in my composition edition 6 responsum 130 to Symiatish to distinguish between one mesalfei lefi tumo and two; so too one may say here, since here it teaches he heard from counter-missionaries plural language — from this that he heard from many, minority of many is two; and with this according to all connecting words are not required; examine and question.`,
  "17#_": `Seif 24 — They found him killed, etc., even though they have prominent signs, etc. Behold in several responsa I relied to be lenient where she left Torah prohibition and is forbidden only rabbinically — rabbinic signs suffice. And behold now I saw for one author in our time who proved from Hagahot Maimoniyot to the contrary from what is stated regarding falling in endless waters they were stringent until forehead and nose — and behold nose is required only rabbinically as stated in Bekhorot that for woman's testimony the rabbis were stringent, etc.; behold this is only blindness of the eye of the viewer — for this is the question in Bekhorot; but afterward the Gemara concludes and alternatively he recognizes alone and facial recognition alone; if so according to that version it is from Torah that nose is also required; and we hold in all versions both; and Hagahot Maimoniyot therefore holds both are required; and in any case this is correct that rabbinically rabbinic signs help; and I did not need to mention this except because it touches what I relied on several times — for this I raised in writing to remove from the reader's heart in his book; examine. And behold in law — dispute of the poskim whether prominent signs help for us on garments that are not worn on the person; see in my composition on Orach Chaim laws of Lulav part the inscribed 95 page 120 in responsum 130 Schwabach what I wrote as proof to permit also for us on garments not worn on the person; and I raised that even rabbinic signs — nevertheless signs that come from reasoning are preferable — see there and examine. And see further what I wrote on the law of rabbinic signs and whether two intermediate signs become prominent — see in my composition Chokhmat HaTorah on the Torah parashat Chayei Sarah year 5622 what I wrote proof for this from Torah incidentally regarding rabbinic signs and two intermediates becoming prominent — see there; may God merit me to aim according to law and enlighten my eyes in the light of Torah — see there and examine. And see in the novellae of Bach on Gittin what he wrote on matters of rabbinic signs — what to note, several with God's help; and what can be brought as proof for what the annotator wrote and I too intended this in several responsa where she left Torah prohibition and prohibition is only rabbinic — rabbinic signs help — see there and examine. And see Beit Shmuel s.k. 70 what he wrote in the name of Mahariw in the name of the Rama that with an intermediate sign if she married she need not leave; and see in my composition on Even HaEzer edition 5 siman 104 in my responsum 130 to Kashinov what I wrote to explain his intent — see there and examine. And see in my composition on Orach Chaim siman 248 (year 5626) what I wrote there in responsum to resolve the difficulty of Even HaEzer on the matter of profane signs for 909 — see there where I extended on this; examine. And it is known the opinion of the poskim who dispute whether two intermediate signs become a prominent sign; and we already spoke much on this with God's help; and now it appears to me proof it is not a prominent sign from what we say in chapter 2 of Bava Metzia that it was asked — are signs Torah signs or rabbinic signs; practical difference to return a woman's get through signs; and it is proven explicitly that if rabbinic signs we do not return a get with an intermediate sign; difficulty: why should it be — we are concerned for two Yishai sons or two Shevurai; at least they are not worse than an intermediate sign, for there is an opinion we are not concerned for two Yishai sons if they were not established, and likewise for two Shevurai — and so we hold; and with signs we hold rabbinic signs — behold an intermediate sign is worse than concern for two Yishai sons or two Shevurai; if so even for the opinion concerned for two Yishai sons and two Shevurai — at least not worse than intermediate sign; if so since there is another opinion they can combine his name and her name and the name of their city with an intermediate sign and it would be like a prominent sign; and even if rabbinic signs — why should he not return? However this could be rejected — one may say the intent of practical difference is where they know another such get was lost in such a name and afterward one get was found and we do not know whose it is — whether he returns it through an intermediate sign; however from tractate Gittin properly one can bring proof from what is stated Gittin 27b — R' Eliezer said such as he said there is a hole next to such-and-such a letter, but a hole in general — I am not precise whether Torah signs or rabbinic; and there the difficulty works well — behold the case is he does not know if there is another Yishai son or two Shevurai there — behold this is the main pilpul of the Gemara there whether we are concerned for two Shevurai; if so it discusses where it is unknown only to fear for him; if so why need prominent sign — even safek they can combine with the name and city name and it would be prominent; at least R' Eliezer should answer thus; perforce it is proven two intermediates do not combine for a prominent sign. And further from what is stated there afterward in Rabbah in Bach that he said I do not know whether because of sign or because of visual identification — specifically for a Torah scholar; and why did he not also say perhaps from both — perhaps visual identification alone does not help and there is no distinction between Torah scholar and other — only because of both we return not because of sign and visual identification together becoming prominent sign; perforce it is compelled that combination does not make a prominent sign; examine.`,
  "18#_": `Seif 27 — They found him killed, etc. Behold the poskim brought the opinion of Mabit and it is brought in Tikkun Soferim to be lenient when the killed one is found on the same road he was lost — based on this presumption like a lost object; and I already used this reasoning in several places. And behold now I wonder on this reasoning from tractate Bekhorot chapter 1 (7b) where it says the reason "I see it swallowed," etc. — if so we see it swallowed we say this one was eaten and this one spawned — difficulty: what does it challenge — let us say it is presumption that the lost one is the found one; perforce it is proven as I wrote in responsum already that we do not say "this is the one that was lost" except where they found him as he was before he was lost; but if he changed somewhat from before he was lost we do not say this presumption; therefore since the Gemara's intent is they did not find him whole — if so it is not as when he was lost; therefore this presumption is inapplicable; therefore it answers they found him whole; if so there is presumption this is the one lost. But from Rashi there s.v. that they found him whole — they found all his form and he is large; and if he were a fetus he would not remain in the womb so long, etc. — implies even when found whole we do not say this is the one lost, etc.; uncertain in this; examine well. And behold in law to say regarding agunah "this one lost is this one found" — see in my composition on Niddah in the omissions on Yoreh De'ah siman 197 page 120 in responsum 130 to Brigel where I raised one may say regarding a matter that has no permission for all we say this one lost even for Rashbag and they do not dispute this at all — see there with God's help; examine.`,
  "19#_": `Seif 32 — But if he fell into the sea, etc., I say it is another man's leg, etc. Behold this is the opinion of the early authorities in Yevamot; but Rambam's opinion is the opposite — even when they brought up a leg simply she is permitted; and it is brought in Beit Yosef briefly their words; and see in my responsum in my edition 7 siman 207 and siman 209 where I wrote it is appropriate to rule like Rambam and the Rashba's words are wondrous in his proof; therefore in any case it is appropriate to be lenient here in a place of agunah — see there and examine.`,
  "20#_": `Seif 34 — And likewise the woman, etc., behold she may not marry, etc. See what I wrote in responsum 130 Tschernobil acquired place incidentally in my composition Turei Zahav edition 7 siman 58 on Yoreh De'ah laws of bekhor siman 309 and in my edition 5 page 148 siman 168 — see there and examine.`,
  "21#_": `Seif 37 — One witness came, etc. See Beit Shmuel s.k. 116 who brought Rashi's view that if she says "died" — even if many disqualified say "did not die" she is believed, for one witness is believed regarding himself; and see what I wrote with God's help in my edition 6 siman 28 in responsum 130 to Kronstein what I wrote to pilpul on this — see there with God's help; examine.`,
  "22#_": `In that seif in the gloss — However because of slander she should not marry. Behold if she married and was widowed or divorced — if she is permitted afterward to marry ab initio; see in my edition 6 siman 1 in responsum to Lemberg laws of Kiddushin siman 37 what I wrote there to prove she is forbidden to marry ab initio even then — see there and examine. And see in my composition Mishpat Tzedek on Choshen Mishpat edition 4 siman 48 laws of lost objects siman 262 what I proved from the Gemara Gittin that two inferior signs combine for an intermediate sign — and all the more per this that two intermediates combine for a prominent sign — see there and examine.`,
  "23#_": `S.k. 38 — A woman says died, etc. See above in Beit Shmuel s.k. 38 what he brought as a difficulty in the name of the Gaon Rabbi Asher on Rivash from Gittin — why did it not challenge there, and perhaps he is a gentile; and see what I wrote on this in the words of Beit Shmuel in responsum 130 to Kamenitz in edition acquired place it is laws of Gittin siman 120. And behold the Gaon's difficulties appear to leave it in uncertainty — for granted regarding so-and-so a snake bit him and he died — here comes proof whether he is Israelite or gentile; therefore he should have challenged perhaps he is gentile; but regarding all who hear a voice write a get for my wife — it is proven he is expert in laws since he did not say simply "write a get for my wife" and specified those who hear his voice; and also said "let them write a get for my wife" and did not say "give a get to my wife" or "write and give" — proven he is expert in laws and it is not the way of a gentile to be expert in laws; therefore no concern perhaps he is gentile — examine and it is simple.`,
  "24#_": `Seif 39 in the gloss — And the court must be three fit, etc. Behold for those who hold specifically permission of three is required — perforce it is not like other instruction of prohibition and permission where the court only informs the law whether to forbid or permit; for then one individual would suffice; perforce they hold speech causes the permission and so Chazal enacted she is permitted only on their word; therefore according to them it appears appropriate ab initio the permission should be before the woman — one may compare this to permission of vows that does not help through an agent, only we require the vow-maker himself come before those who permit; and this is because in permission of vows their speech causes the permission; therefore it does not help through an agent; so too here their speech causes the permission and with their mouths she is permitted — we require she be before the woman ab initio and not not in her presence — so is my humble opinion in this; examine.`,
  "25#_": `Seif 42 — Two witnesses say died, etc. See in Choshen Mishpat and Beit Shmuel that even if she married with permission she must leave; and this is from the Gemara Yevamot 88; and see in my edition 6 siman 1 second responsum in my responsum 130 to Laros in laws of Kiddushin siman 37 what I wrote to pilpul on this and that this is not compelled — see there and several matters belonging to the sugya of two say died — see there also regarding law of one witness says died and two say did not die — we wrote there and in resolving the sugya and the words of Peri Chadash 101 and Neta Sha'ashuim siman 75 and 77 — see there with God's help; examine. And behold what should be noted on this in the words of Rambam laws of inadvertent sins chapter 8 law 3 — see in my composition on Orach Chaim laws of Yom Kippur part the inscribed 102 page 66 what I wrote to resolve this there — see there and examine. And behold what should be noted in this sugya in what the Gemara challenges "who is so brazen," etc., and Rav Hamnuna said, etc. — and this contradicts the Gemara Nedarim; see in my responsum on laws of Gittin siman 141 incidentally what I wrote on this — and it is in my edition 7 siman 99 — see there and examine.`,
  "26#_": `Seif 48 in the gloss — Or he apostatized, etc. See what I wrote in my edition 6 siman 19 in responsum 130 to Przemysl acquired place in this section — see there what I wrote on the reason of the matter and how the poskim dispute on this — see there also what we wrote with one witness for a husband's death that he was a fool also depends on this — see there with God's help; examine.`,
  "27#_": `Seif 55 — There was a plague in the world, etc. See what I wrote in responsum 130 to Tulchin acquired place on Even HaEzer laws of chalitzah where I pilpuled much on this law and on the presumption it is war in the world and in resolving Tosafot's difficulties on this — and it is in my edition 7 siman 56; see there what I wrote with God's help; examine.`,
  "28#_": `Seif 58 — A woman who was betrothed and erred, etc. Behold from Rama's language that wrote "she must leave from this one and that one" — it implies she requires a get from the second; and in my opinion this law is very uncertain. Behold the source of this law is from chapter HaIshah Rabba — they decreed lest they say the first divorced and the second married; and in truth it is explicit there that it discusses her husband went to a distant land and they came and told her your husband died, etc.; per this one may say specifically in such a case — since she married based on one witness or two witnesses and from presumption they do not come to court to testify and she married based on complete permission of court — here there is concern that even if he comes and they say the first divorced and the second married — they will say the court certainly dayika and would not permit her except because they know the first divorced; but in her error thinking she was not betrothed — since it was not done by court nor by witnesses nor was the matter publicized that she married with permission from the outset — they will err to say the first divorced and the second married; and whence to be stringent here in a rabbinic matter — since the get from the second is only rabbinic; and proof for this directly from what is stated above in this siman seif 1 — "a married woman is among the forbidden relations and kiddushin do not take effect in her," etc.; and behold since afterward he mentioned rabbinic get in the end and in the beginning did not mention thus — it is proven kiddushin do not take effect even rabbinically; perforce it discusses she was betrothed not in her husband's presence — for if in his presence behold he wrote afterward she is betrothed to the second for a woman does not act brazenly, etc.; perforce it discusses not in her husband's presence; and so there is proof from siman 46 seif 5 — "the kiddushin of the second are nothing" proves she does not need a get at all even rabbinically; and why are we not concerned lest the first divorced, etc. — perforce in such a case we are not concerned for this. However these proofs — behold it is explicit in Shulchan Aruch above seif 57 that specifically when she married she needs a get from the second and not when only betrothed; nevertheless although we have no proof — since the Gemara and poskim specify specifically in such a case when her husband went to a distant land and they told her your husband died — and it does not teach when her husband is here and she went and married another — that she needs a get from the second; perforce in such a case she does not need a get from the second; and whence to be stringent in such a case. And even Rama who is stringent — it appears this is specifically in her error since in any case she married with permission and the matter was publicized — there is concern they will say the first divorced since she married with permission; but if she married another wantonly — it appears certainly she does not need a get from the second; and even though she excuses herself now that they told her at first her husband died — she is not believed to compel him to give her a get to be forbidden to her relatives; for what Chazal believed her to say her husband died is because of presumption dayika uminsaba; but since she already married and it is known her husband is alive — it appears she did not dayika; if so on the contrary the presumption of dayika supports the opposite that they did not tell her he died since she married wantonly; therefore she has no power to compel him to give a get — only she is forbidden for she made herself a piece of prohibition; per his words she needs a get from the second rabbinically and he is not obligated to give her a get — only we request from him as the explicit law in siman 48. And behold proof for my words from Yevamot 72 regarding a woman who did not wait after her husband three hours — and we establish it in erroneous kiddushin; and see Rashi there she does not need a get; nevertheless since it is the way of marriage the rabbis did not decree; difficulty: why not establish she married the first husband literally and they told her your husband died and she married another within three hours, or erred and thought she was not betrothed and married another — in this she needs a get from this one and that one; therefore they did not decree since it is rabbinic marriage and requires a get; nevertheless he is not profaned from the second — one may say she became pregnant from first intercourse and is not yet a zona; and he is not profaned, for he is profaned only from those disqualified from kehunah; as stated explicitly in Shulchan Aruch siman 7 seif 14 that one who has relations with forbidden relations in first intercourse is not profaned except from mamzer; therefore he does not defile them. And we say that wherever she does not need a get at all — they properly decreed only where she needs rabbinic get — then they did not decree; for either way from Torah even in promiscuity he is kohen and the rabbis decreed; and rabbinically behold it is marriage — why not answer thus; perforce she does not need a get from the second only when he went to a distant land and not when the husband is here; and when he went to a distant land it is not common that the time would be so close from his departure until the second marriage that there would not be three hours from the day of his departure until the second marriage; and there would be witnesses in the middle who told her he died; therefore it does not establish such a case; and still uncertain in resolving the above difficulty; however for the law it appears as I wrote; and even in this I do not decide — perhaps God will merit me another time to explain more on this; examine well.`,
  "29#_": `There in that seif in the gloss — Or the court instructed her, etc. See in Taz what he wondered on this law and in Beit Shmuel; and see in my composition on Even HaEzer edition 4 in my responsum 130 to Timashpol what I answered the Rashba's words on the correct — see there and examine.`,
};
